 ## 2026-09-03
 
+### 1338 — A figure published beside a measurement must be derived from it, not restated next to it: third instance in one artifact
+
+- SCOPE: `README.md` (one figure corrected), new `internal/traceability/readme_figures_pin_test.go`, `internal/cliresult/client_test.go` (two new sweeps), `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` and its projection digest. Round-4 rework of TASK-260830-33sfxc after review; §14.2, §15.1, §15.2. No production code changed — every round-4 finding was an evidence defect, not a behaviour defect.
+- ROOT CAUSE: `README.md` published "73 executable acceptance cases" while `ownership.v0.5.0.json` held 74 entries with 74 distinct ids and `tracecheck` printed `acceptance_cases=74`. The first pass of this leaf moved the registry from 65 to 73 and updated the sentence; the round-2 rework added a case and did not. Nothing measured the figure — setting it to 999 left `go test ./...` and `tracecheck` both at exit 0.
+- FINDING: this is the THIRD occurrence of the identical class inside this one artifact, and the first two are recorded here by the same author. Entry 1015: the "nineteen §15.2 rows" claim against an eighteen-row table. Entry 1157: two of four published fan-in rows wrong, root cause named at the time as "prose nobody measured". The response to the second was `TestREADMEFanInTableIsDerivedFromTheMeasuredProjection`, which parses the README and re-derives every cell — and it sits two hundred lines from a sentence that restates instead of derives and is off by one. The tool for the class existed, in the repository, written for this exact failure, and was never pointed at the neighbouring paragraph.
+- DECISION: the general rule, worth stating once. A figure published beside a measurement must be an INPUT to the test, not a second copy of the answer. Three instances in one artifact is not carelessness; it is the natural failure mode of prose sitting near data. The only fix that holds is parsing the prose and re-deriving every number in it from the thing it describes.
+- FIX: `TestREADMEOwnershipFiguresAreDerivedFromTheMeasuredReport` locates the paragraph by its heading, refuses a heading it finds twice or not at all, and re-derives all seven figures from `traceability.VerifyRepository` — not only the one that drifted, because the other six were correct today and unmeasured for exactly the same reason. Each figure is located by the noun phrase naming what it counts, so re-ordering the clause cannot re-point a pin at a different number.
+- DECISION: the pin polices its own completeness. Every digit run in the paragraph that no pinned phrase consumed is reported as unmeasured, with only dotted version and path tokens exempt under a deliberately narrow rule. That makes DELETING a row from the pinned-figure list a red rather than a quietly smaller comparison — the failure mode entry 2113 records for a derived gate that did not police its own extraction pattern.
+- FINDING (reviewer Observation A, acted on): `exitStatusIsNotEnough` documents its own count as "a measured count rather than as advice" and `README.md` repeated the claim, but every assertion on those refusals matched the trailing clause `identifies no failure` and nothing read the number. Replacing `len(fanIn[exitStatus])` with a constant, or the whole `%d registered` clause with the word "many", left the suite green. Both are killed now, and so is the narrowing that keeps the count correct at exit 6 only — which is exactly the single assertion proposed as the minimal fix. The sweep drives every registered command at every registered §15.2 failure status through `Read` and re-derives each answer from `CodesByExitStatus`.
+- FINDING (reviewer Observation B, acted on): retry independence from the exit status was proven at exit 12 alone, and the one exit-15 envelope any test drove already declared `retryable: true`, so a fabricated "if the status is 15, retry" branch returned the answer the document would have given and survived. `TestTheRetryDecisionFollowsTheDocumentAtEveryFailureStatus` drives both polarities across the whole failure domain; the true arm covers fourteen of seventeen statuses because the pinned document forbids the claim for every code of the other three, and that figure is pinned rather than left implicit.
+- DECISION (reviewer Observation C, left alone): the two survivors the round-3 reviewer checked as genuinely equivalent — `Succeeded()` recomputed from `exitStatus`, and `ExitStatus()` returned from `failure.ExitCode()` — are NOT "fixed". They survive because `readSuccess` and `readFailure` between them make document and status provably agree on every admitted reading. A survivor dismissed with a reason is evidence; one quietly fixed hides why it survived. The same holds for the two round-2 dismissals, the near-equivalent `details` narrowing of `requireCommonDataModel` and the `CodesByExitStatus` empty-group pre-seed.
+- ANOMALY: my first hand-probe of the "many" mutant reported a non-zero exit and I read it as a kill. It was a BUILD failure — dropping the `%d` argument left `fanIn` declared and not used. The harness's `NOT-COMPILED` arm caught it on the next run. A mutant that does not compile proves nothing about the test, and a non-zero exit status is not by itself a kill.
+- EVIDENCE: harness grown from 28 to 44 mutants — 43 killed, 1 declared subsumed (unchanged), 0 unexplained survivors, suite restored green. Supersedes the "28 mutants at 27 killed" figure in the 1231 entry below.
+- DECISION: the "narrows rather than deletes" figure is no longer counted by hand either. The class is now DEFINED in the harness — enforcement left in place for part of the domain, whether a restricted class, one of several redundant copies, a reordering, or a published claim kept and given a specific wrong value — and the count computed from that definition, with the harness failing closed if the set names a mutant that does not exist. Applied to the 28-mutant harness the definition selects exactly the fourteen that revision published; the round-4 count is twenty-seven. Same lesson as the entry title, applied to my own evidence prose rather than to somebody else's.
+- BOUND, stated rather than glossed: the harness mutates the working tree, so it lives under `.temp/` and is attached to the board item. `go test ./...` does not run it and no committed artifact derives its counts. `README.md` now says so where it publishes them, instead of letting a harness figure sit beside gated ones and read as though it were gated too.
